WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.753
Secure C review should focus on data flow,
ownership, length checks, cleanup,

2
00:00:08.833 --> 00:00:11.209
and failure behavior.

3
00:00:11.289 --> 00:00:19.042
Review where input enters, how it is
validated, how sizes are calculated,

4
00:00:19.122 --> 00:00:27.250
and where data is copied, formatted,
stored, or passed to another component.

5
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:34.207
Review every allocation, every free, every
ownership transfer,

6
00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:39.164
and every pointer stored beyond a local
scope.

7
00:00:39.244 --> 00:00:46.497
A pointer saved into a structure,
callback, global variable, cache,

8
00:00:46.577 --> 00:00:52.454
or thread context may outlive the scope
that created it.

9
00:00:52.534 --> 00:01:00.412
The code should show who owns the object
and who is responsible for cleanup.

10
00:01:00.492 --> 00:01:04.493
Review boundary handling carefully.

11
00:01:04.573 --> 00:01:10.826
Test empty input, maximum allowed sizes,
too-large input,

12
00:01:10.906 --> 00:01:19.034
negative numbers, malformed records,
embedded delimiters, partial reads,

13
00:01:19.114 --> 00:01:24.616
interrupted operations, and allocation
failures.

14
00:01:24.696 --> 00:01:33.824
These cases often reveal assumptions that
ordinary happy-path tests do not exercise.

15
00:01:33.904 --> 00:01:36.655
Failure testing matters.

16
00:01:36.735 --> 00:01:38.861
A file open can fail.

17
00:01:38.941 --> 00:01:41.192
A read can be partial.

18
00:01:41.272 --> 00:01:45.523
A memory allocation can return failure.

19
00:01:45.603 --> 00:01:50.105
A parser can stop halfway through a
record.

20
00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:54.186
A cleanup call can encounter an error.

21
00:01:54.266 --> 00:02:00.394
The design should define which failures
are recoverable,

22
00:02:00.474 --> 00:02:07.351
which should stop processing, and what
state remains afterward.

23
00:02:07.431 --> 00:02:13.308
Review logging and errors for sensitive
data exposure.

24
00:02:13.388 --> 00:02:19.515
C code that handles raw buffers, paths,
keys, device data,

25
00:02:19.595 --> 00:02:28.474
or protocol records should not dump
arbitrary memory or raw input into routine logs.

26
00:02:28.554 --> 00:02:37.432
Safe diagnostics preserve enough context
for operators without turning logs into a

27
00:02:37.512 --> 00:02:39.638
data exposure path.

28
00:02:39.718 --> 00:02:46.470
Review concurrency assumptions if buffers,
handles, globals,

29
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:54.553
or shared structures can be accessed by
multiple threads, signal handlers,

30
00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:58.385
interrupt handlers, or callbacks.

31
00:02:58.465 --> 00:03:07.343
Secure C review is strongest when it uses
checklists to make these habits repeatable

32
00:03:07.423 --> 00:03:13.300
rather than relying on one expert to
notice every issue.
